  dailyRead: "This is a Sunday report, so the first thing it owes is honesty about what it can and cannot know. No major has traded since Friday's close, there has been no new macro print from any of the eight economies, and every policy rate on the board is exactly where TradingEconomics had it yesterday. That means no score on this board has earned a change on price, and the ones that stay put stay put for that reason rather than from inertia. Friday's tape, re-read from TradingEconomics this morning: the dollar index closed at 103.4159, up 0.24% on the day, 0.84% on the month and 4.15% on the year, a 19-month high and the best G10 performer of the week on MUFG's ranking. EUR/USD 1.1017, minus 0.28% and minus 5.19% on the year. GBP/USD 1.3203, minus 0.17% but plus 0.08% on the month and plus 0.23% on the year. USD/JPY 159.645, minus 0.08%, the yen the only major to take ground off the dollar on the day. AUD/USD 0.6771, minus 0.54%. NZD/USD 0.5519, minus 0.43%. USD/CAD 1.4266, plus 0.09%. USD/CHF 0.8385, plus 0.43%, which makes the franc the weakest major of the session. The curve: US 10-year 5.15% and up a basis point, 30-year 5.47%, 2-year 4.86% and down three, and the 10-year TIPS real yield at 2.88%, up four basis points - a shade lower than the 2.90% this file carried on Friday, and TradingEconomics wins. What the weekend genuinely added is a better explanation of the weakest currency on the board, and it is a correction of emphasis this file should make plainly. For a week the Aussie's problem has been described here as copper, and copper is real - 6.08 dollars a pound, minus 1.67% on Friday and minus 8.05% on the month. But the bigger leg is policy, and it is hiding behind a hawkish-looking number. The Reserve Bank of Australia raised the cash rate to 4.85% on November 3, the fifth increase of the year and the highest since late 2008, and the market sold the currency anyway. The reason is in the statement rather than the decision: the explicit reference to further increases was removed, three of the nine members voted to hold outright, and the bank's own guidance now has inflation reaching the top of the 2 to 3 per cent band only in late 2027 while growth slows, housing prices fall and unemployment rises more than previously expected. MUFG's read is that the Aussie's support from pre-emptive tightening is fading, and price agrees: 0.6771 is a 10-month low and Friday broke a multi-month consolidation that had held since late January. A central bank at an 18-year-high policy rate that has just stopped promising more is a worse currency story than a central bank at 2.75% that is still promising, which is exactly the Australia-New Zealand setup and why AUD/NZD stays the highest-conviction cross here. Tuesday's RBA minutes at 02:30 and Governor Bullock at 00:50 are where that thesis gets confirmed or broken, and Thursday's October employment at 02:30, forecast plus 25,200 after September's minus 39,329, is the two-way risk. The second thing worth stating is a reorder at the top. The pound moves above the euro in the strength table, and for a specific reason rather than a view: sterling is the only non-dollar major flat on the month, plus 0.08% against a dollar index up 0.84%, and it has a December 17 decision that is still genuinely live after the November 5 hike to 4.00% on an 8-1 vote with Catherine Mann dissenting for a half-point. The euro's December 17 hike, by contrast, is already fully owned - all 71 economists in the Reuters poll taken November 10 to 12 expect the deposit rate to go to 2.75%, and most of them describe it as the last of the cycle. A priced endpoint is worth less to a currency than an unpriced path, which is the same argument that keeps the dollar at plus two. It cuts against sterling on Wednesday, though, and that detail matters: consensus for October UK CPI is 3.6% while the Bank's own November projection implied about 3.73%, so an in-line print is mildly soft against the Bank's forecast, not hot. Third, the week's catalyst that is not on any red-folder list. Nvidia reports its October quarter after Tuesday's US close, so the reaction lands in Wednesday's Asian session, and options are pricing a move of about plus or minus 10.6% against a ten-year earnings-day average of 7.8%. That matters here because the Huang resignation - the board accepted it on November 11, chief financial officer Colette Kress is interim chief executive, Harvey Jones is the company's first board chair - is the reason silver broke under 50 dollars while gold held: forced selling out of a tech rout, not a rates move. Silver at 48.72, minus 2.59% and a one-year low, versus gold at 3,881.75, plus 0.14% on the same day. If Tuesday's print is bad, that channel reopens and silver, the DAX semis and the Aussie all take it; if it is good, the cleanest casualty is the gold and silver short. Fourth, a nuance on Monday that is easy to get wrong. The Fed chair speaks at 19:00 and ForexFactory has it as a red folder, but the event is a Productivity and Jobs Task Force conference in San Francisco on the economics of artificial intelligence, and Warsh said at the October IMF meetings that he does not believe in saying in advance what he might do at the remaining meetings. Expect an AI-and-inflation argument rather than a December or January signal, and treat the real Fed event of the week as Wednesday's October 28 minutes at 21:00, which will show how wide the hawkish group actually is behind Cook's and Hammack's dissents. The dollar case itself is unchanged and worth restating in its narrower, more defensible form: December is roughly 85% to 89% priced on CME-based measures after the hot October CPI, the half-point has no trigger left, and the trade is the path rather than the increment. The January pricing this file quoted on Friday at 57.5% could not be re-verified over a closed weekend, so it is carried as Friday's reading rather than today's fact. Finally the two quiet ones with live tests. Canada goes first, at 15:30 Monday, with CPI m/m forecast plus 0.3% from plus 0.1% and the median and trimmed cores forecast at 2.0% and 1.9% - the Bank of Canada hiked to 2.50% in October for the first time in three years and said there could be a need to go further while cautioning, so this print is what makes December 9 live or dead, against an unemployment rate at a six-month high of 6.7% and a third consecutive fall in employment. And Japan's Q3 GDP lands tonight at 01:50, forecast plus 0.1% quarter on quarter, downgraded by ForexFactory to a low folder but still the last large domestic input before the December 18 meeting at which a Reuters-poll majority expects 1.50%; the yen's problem is not the hike, it is that 400 basis points of carry and a 160 intervention line cancel each other out, and Tokyo core CPI at 2.2% in November already beat its 2.1% forecast without moving the pair.",
