    EUR: { next: "hike", when: "December 17 at 15:15, the final meeting of 2026 and, on the economists' consensus, the final hike of the cycle", note: "The deposit facility rate is 2.50% on TradingEconomics - the main refinancing rate is 2.65% and is not the policy rate for this board - after an unanimous October hold at which Lagarde confirmed the council discussed a hike. All 71 economists in the Reuters poll taken November 10 to 12 expect 2.75% on December 17, and most describe it as the last of the cycle, with a bit over a third seeing one more in early 2027. That is a priced endpoint rather than an unpriced path, which is why the euro sits behind the pound this morning. Headline HICP is 4.0%, a three-year high, against core steady at 2.5%, so the hike is being bought with energy. Lagarde speaks three times this week - Wednesday 16:25, Thursday 15:30 and Friday 10:15 - and is reported to be leaving early, in February 2027, though she has not resigned." },
    GBP: { next: "hike", when: "December 17 at 14:00, still genuinely live, with Wednesday's CPI at 09:00 the thing that moves it", note: "Bank Rate is 4.00% after the November 5 increase, voted 8-1 with Catherine Mann dissenting for a half-point to 4.25% - the first rise since August 2023. The Bank now projects CPI rising to around 5% by the middle of next year from 3.5% before energy effects fade, and said activity data have generally been stronger than expected. Bailey has signalled another increase may be needed unless inflation risks ease while warning against reading November as the start of a long series, and Lombardelli has kept December open without committing. The detail that matters Wednesday: consensus for October CPI is 3.6% while the Bank's own November projection implied about 3.73%, so an in-line print is mildly soft against the Bank, not hot. Bailey speaks twice that day, 09:30 and 17:00." },
    JPY: { next: "hike", when: "December 18 at 05:00, with tonight's Q3 GDP at 01:50 the nearer test and intervention the nearer event risk", note: "The policy rate is 1.25% after an 8-1 October hold, with Hajime Takata dissenting for a faster move toward neutral, following consecutive quarter-point hikes in June and September that took borrowing costs to their highest since April 1995. The bank said underlying CPI inflation has aligned with the 2% target for the first time since 2013 and kept FY2026 inflation at 2.5% while lifting FY2027 to 2.6%. A Reuters poll majority expects 1.50% on December 18 and a Bloomberg survey had 83% on December, with Kameda looking for roughly quarterly steps to a 2% terminal. Tokyo core CPI ran 2.2% in November against a 2.1% forecast. Against that: 400 basis points of carry, a 160 intervention line the Ministry of Finance defended with a record 15.4 trillion yen between July 30 and August 3, and nearly three-quarters of a Reuters panel expecting another dissent at the next hike to weigh on the yen." },
    AUD: { next: "hold", when: "December 8 at 05:30, now the base case rather than a close call, with three domestic tests before it from Tuesday 02:30", note: "The cash rate is 4.85% after the November 3 increase, carried 6-3 with three members voting to hold - the fifth hike of the year and the highest since late 2008. The decisive detail is what was taken out rather than what was done: the explicit reference to further increases was removed, and the bank's own guidance has inflation reaching the top of the 2 to 3 per cent band only in late 2027 while growth slows further, housing prices fall significantly and unemployment rises more than previously anticipated. MUFG reads the currency's support from pre-emptive tightening as fading, and AUD/USD at a 10-month low agrees. Tuesday brings Bullock at 00:50 and the November minutes at 02:30, Wednesday the Q3 wage price index at 02:30 forecast plus 0.9%, and Thursday October employment at 02:30 forecast plus 25,200 after minus 39,329." },
    NZD: { next: "hike", when: "December 9 at 03:00, a full Monetary Policy Statement, with Tuesday's inflation expectations at 04:00 the direct input", note: "The official cash rate is 2.75% after October's hold, taken 4-3 against market pricing that had been near 90% for a hike - Breman, Hansen, Williams and Gai held, while Gourley, Conway and Silk voted to go. The committee said tighter policy is likely to be required, depending on the outlook, and flagged near-term inflation uncertainty from volatile oil and fuel prices. Standard Chartered, OCBC and UOB all look for 3.00% in December with another in early 2027. Headline CPI is 3.7% and falling, but the bank's own track has it worsening to about 4.4% in Q4, which is the unusual feature here: a central bank forecasting its own inflation higher before it improves. Tuesday's Q4 inflation expectations at 04:00 is a red folder and the cleanest read on December; the Financial Stability Report lands Monday 22:00 and Governor Breman speaks Wednesday 20:00." },
    CAD: { next: "hike", when: "December 9 at 16:45, a regular decision with no Monetary Policy Report, and Monday's CPI at 15:30 decides whether it is live", note: "The overnight rate is 2.50% after October's quarter-point increase, the first rise in three years, taken to reduce the risk that high energy prices spread into other goods and services. The bank said there could be a need to raise further although caution is necessary, called inflation higher and more persistent than in the July Report, and now sees CPI averaging 3% in 2027, a full point above July, before returning to 2% in mid-2028, with growth of 1% in 2026 and 2% in each of 2027 and 2028. Monday's October CPI is the swing factor: m/m forecast plus 0.3% from plus 0.1%, median 2.0% from 1.9%, trimmed 1.9% from 1.8%. Set against it, unemployment at a six-month high of 6.7%, a third consecutive fall in employment, and WTI at 88.56 and minus 6.87% on the month. Gravelle speaks Wednesday 19:30." },
    CHF: { next: "hold", when: "December 10 at 10:30, the quarterly assessment, with a hike not expected", note: "The SNB policy rate is 0.00%, unchanged since mid-2025 and held again in September, with the bank saying the stance remains appropriate and that it stands willing to intervene in foreign exchange as necessary. It forecasts average inflation of 0.7% in 2026 and 0.8% in each of 2027 and 2028 and GDP growth of 1.5% to 2% this year. October CPI is 1.5%, close to a three-year high, but core is only 0.5%, so the rise is energy and the bank has been explicit that it discounts it - Schlegel says price pressures remain contained and the recent fall in oil moderates the risk further. Previews into December are near-unanimous for another hold, and the September meeting saw the bank push back against market pricing of 50 to 75 basis points of hikes over twelve months. The franc's problem is not the rate gap alone: it was not bid in a genuine equity scare." }
